.TH WMTUBE 1 "October 2026" "wmtube 0.3" "Window Maker dockapps"
.SH NAME
wmtube \- play YouTube and PeerTube videos inside a Window Maker dockapp
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmtube
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIURL\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wmtube
is a 64x64 dockapp that streams a YouTube, PeerTube (any instance) or other
yt-dlp supported video inside its tile. The 16:9 video fills a 56x32 band;
the rows above and below can show a scrolling title and the elapsed / total
play time in the classic LED dockapp style.
.PP
Playlist links (including YouTube Music playlists) play their tracks in
order. Between tracks the video area goes blank and the title shows
.B LOADING
until the next one starts; album-only tracks show their cover art.
.PP
Internet radio and other live streams (e.g. Icecast / SHOUTcast URLs) play
with a blank video area and elapsed time only; the title follows the
station's "now playing" information as songs change.
.PP
Playback is done by libmpv, which uses
.BR yt-dlp (1)
to resolve the URL. A low-resolution stream is requested by default to keep
CPU and bandwidth use small.
.I ~/.local/bin
(where pipx installs yt-dlp) is put first in
.B PATH
at startup, so a current pipx yt-dlp is used even when the window manager
was started without it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-display " display"
X display to connect to.
.TP
.BI \-mode " both|title|time|none"
Which text rows to show initially (default \fBboth\fR).
.TP
.BI \-fg " color"
Text colour (default #20B2AE).
.TP
.BI \-bg " color"
Background colour (default #202020).
.TP
.B \-mute
Start muted.
.TP
.B \-loop
Loop the video forever.
.TP
.B \-tracknum
When playing a playlist, append the track position to the title, as
\fITITLE\fR [\fIM\fR/\fIN\fR]. Off by default.
.TP
.BI \-ytdl\-format " format"
yt-dlp format selector (default prefers 144p H.264 plus best audio).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help
Show a short help text.
.SH MOUSE
.TP
.B Left click
Pause / resume. When a video ends the tile returns to its idle state.
When no video is
loaded, toggles the scrolling help text instead (hidden at startup) (and clears any error
message), so an idle docked tile can sit still.
.TP
.B Double click
Open the video full size in a separate \fBmpv\fR at the current position and
pause the dockapp.
.TP
.B Middle click
Play the URL currently in the PRIMARY selection (or, failing that, the
CLIPBOARD). Only http:// and https:// URLs (or a bare youtube.com / youtu.be
address) are accepted from the selection, so PeerTube links need their full
https:// URL; local files and other mpv
protocols must be given on the command line.
.TP
.B Right click
Cycle the text rows: both, title only, time only, none.
.TP
.B Shift + left click / Shift + right click
Seek back / forward 10 seconds.
.TP
.B Ctrl + left click / Ctrl + right click
Previous / next track when playing a playlist; does nothing at either end of
the playlist or for a single video.
.TP
.B Ctrl + Shift + left click
Stop playback, clear the playlist and return to the startup state: help
hidden, text rows as given by \fB\-mode\fR, volume 100 (muted only if
started with \fB\-mute\fR).
.TP
.B Scroll wheel
Volume up / down; the level is shown briefly in the time row.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B NO YT-DLP FOUND
Neither yt-dlp nor youtube-dl is in
.BR PATH .
Install a current yt-dlp with
.BR "pipx install yt-dlp" .
.TP
.B ... - TRY PIPX UPGRADE YT-DLP
yt-dlp failed in a way that usually means the site changed and yt-dlp is
out of date, for example
.I The page needs to be reloaded
or
.IR "Unable to extract" .
Run
.B pipx upgrade yt-dlp
and play the video again. Distribution packages of yt-dlp, including
Debian's, are usually too old to work with YouTube. If a packaged copy is
also installed, check which one wmtube finds with
.B which -a yt-dlp
and
.BR "yt-dlp --version" ;
a pipx copy in
.I ~/.local/bin
wins, since wmtube puts that directory first in
.BR PATH .
.PP
Other errors, such as
.IR "This video is unavailable" ,
come from the site and are shown without the hint. Run wmtube from a
terminal to see the full mpv and yt-dlp messages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mpv (1),
.BR yt-dlp (1),
.BR wmaker (1)
